---
import GuideLayout from "../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
---
<GuideLayout
  title="How To Use"
  description="Search the directory, read a record, deploy your own instance."
>
  <h2>1. Search the directory</h2>
  <p>
    Open the <a href="/#directory">Directory</a> on the homepage and search by
    name or specialty, or narrow the list with the industry and risk-level
    filters. The count above the results updates as you type.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Read a record</h2>
  <p>
    Click a row to expand it. Each record shows its computed score, risk
    level, and a per-source breakdown — which of the eight registries
    returned a hit, and which came back clean or unreachable. A record with
    no coverage from a source isn't necessarily clean; it may mean that
    source has nothing on file for that name.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Treat CRITICAL and HIGH as a starting point, not a verdict</h2>
  <p>
    The score is a prioritization signal, not a legal conclusion. A CRITICAL
    rating means the record warrants a closer manual read before retention —
    it doesn't mean the expert should be excluded automatically. Always
    verify a flagged item against the primary source before acting on it.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Point it at your own experts</h2>
  <p>
    The directory reads from <code>expert_data.json</code>, generated by
    <code>gatekeeper_backend_enhanced.py</code>. To vet your own list, add
    names to the script, run it, and the site picks up the refreshed file
    with no rebuild required — see <code>deploy.sh</code> / <code>deploy.bat</code>
    for the one-command version.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Deploy your own instance</h2>
  <p>
    The site is a static Astro build with no server or CMS. Run
    <code>npm run build</code> and deploy the <code>dist/</code> folder, or
    use <code>./deploy.sh</code> to fetch fresh data and push to Vercel in one
    step. Full detail is on the homepage's <a href="/#deploy">Deploy</a> section.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
